1. Download best.pt from Google Colab

Just like you downloaded the .tflite file earlier, you will grab the .pt file from the exact same location in your Colab environment.
Open the Folder icon on the left side of your Colab notebook.
Click through the folders: runs -> detect -> train -> weights.
Look for the file named best.pt (this is the standard PyTorch format, which is ideal for running on a computer webcam rather than a phone).
Click the three dots next to it and select Download.

2. Set Up Your Antigravity IDE Workspace

Because Google Antigravity is a heavily modified fork of Visual Studio Code, it operates using standard workspace folders.
Create a new folder on your computer's desktop (e.g., Petrosains_Webcam).
Open Google Antigravity IDE and open that new folder so it becomes your active workspace.
Move the best.pt file you just downloaded from your computer's Downloads folder directly into this new Petrosains_Webcam folder.

3. Create the Python Script

Inside the Antigravity IDE file explorer, create a new file and name it webcam_detect.py.
Paste the webcam Python code I gave you in the previous message into this file.
Ensure the script and best.pt are sitting next to each other in the exact same folder, then save the file.

4. Run the Code in the Terminal

Antigravity IDE includes a command-line interface and an integrated terminal.
Open the terminal inside Antigravity IDE (using Ctrl + ~ or the top menu).
Install the necessary computer vision tools by typing this command and hitting Enter:

Bash
pip install opencv-python ultralytics
Once the installation finishes, launch your webcam AI by typing:

Bash
python webcam_detect.py
